    "modal.js:58-67 -- the backdrop-click guard (`pressedOnBackdrop`) is a single shared boolean set on `pointerdown` and read on the paired `click`. It correctly handles the documented click-and-drag-out case, but it is not keyed by pointerId: if a second pointer fires `pointerdown` on the panel between an initial backdrop `pointerdown` and its `click` (multi-touch, or a pointer that never completes a click, e.g. pointercancel/long-press), the shared flag can be left in the wrong state for the next click. This is a narrow, hard-to-force edge case (requires overlapping pointers) rather than a single-pointer reachable bug, so it is not raised as a primary finding, but a per-pointerId map (or ignoring non-primary pointers) would remove the shared-mutable-state dependency entirely.",
    "carousel.js:33-42 -- `render()` sets `slide.inert = true` on the outgoing slide synchronously at click time, before the 400ms CSS transition completes, rather than deferring to the `transitionend` handler that already exists for unlocking. This is a defensible tradeoff (it also prevents Tab from entering a slide that is already animating off-screen), but it means a slide that currently holds keyboard focus gets that focus forcibly kicked to `<body>` the instant Next/Prev is clicked, before the user sees it leave. Worth a deliberate call rather than an incidental one; not flagged as a bug because the alternative (deferring inert to transitionend) trades one focus-timing issue for another."
  ],
  "testing_gaps": [
    "Only the pure helpers in src/js/lib/geometry.js are unit tested (tests/geometry.test.mjs). None of navbar.js, scroll.js, carousel.js, or modal.js -- the modules that actually own the timers, rAF loops, and event-driven unlock paths this diff's intent calls out -- have any automated coverage, so a regression in the fallback-timer paths or the transitionend/animationend target-filtering guards would not be caught by CI.",
    "No test exercises scroll.js's interrupt contract: that wheel/touchstart/keydown mid-animation cancels the in-flight scroll, or that a second click on a different nav link cancels the first animation and restarts cleanly (frameId reset, interrupt listeners not double-registered).",
    "No test exercises the carousel's or modal's fallback-timer path directly (i.e., simulating a dropped transitionend/animationend and confirming the 700ms / 400ms timeout still unlocks/closes), which is exactly the scenario the comments say the fallback exists for.",
